Yes — I would call this a strong ML analysis, but I would rate it around 7.5–8/10 for an ATO production analysis, not yet 10/10.

The reason is that you are going beyond simply saying “my model accuracy is 90%.” You are looking at threshold behavior + precision/recall + calibration + Brier score, which is much more appropriate for a risk model. Precision/recall and threshold selection are especially useful when the positive class is relatively rare.

What makes your analysis strong

1. Threshold analysis — strong ✅

You discovered:

Best F1 threshold ≈ 0.1993

and compared it with:

Default 0.5 → Precision 84.6%, Recall 58.4%, F1 69.1%

That’s valuable because you’re asking:

“Where should the business actually operate the model?”

rather than blindly accepting 0.5.

⸻

2. Precision vs recall — strong ✅

You’re recognizing the ATO trade-off:

Catch more attacks ↔ create more customer friction/false alerts.

That’s exactly the type of trade-off a fraud/risk team needs to understand.

⸻

3. Calibration analysis — very strong ✅

Many ML projects stop at F1/AUC.

You went further and asked:

“Can I actually trust a score of 0.70?”

That’s a much more mature question.

A calibrated model should mean that predictions around 0.70 actually correspond to roughly 70% observed positive outcomes.

⸻

4. Brier score — good ✅

You have:

Brier = 0.12271

That’s useful as a probabilistic-quality metric, although don’t interpret 0.12271 as universally “good” or “bad” by itself. Brier score depends on the problem and class distribution and captures more than calibration alone.

