But here’s what would make it 10/10

For your ATO project, I would add these five things:

1. PR-AUC / Average Precision

Especially because ATO is likely highly imbalanced.

Don’t rely primarily on accuracy or even ROC-AUC.

Add:

PR-AUC

⸻

2. Confusion matrix at the actual operating threshold

For example:

	Actual ATO	Actual Legit
Flagged	TP	FP
Not flagged	FN	TN

Then say:

At threshold 0.20, we catch X% of ATO while generating Y alerts per 10,000 logins.

That’s much more meaningful to the business.

⸻

3. Alert rate

Your first screenshot already has:

Alert rate

This is extremely important.

Imagine:

Threshold 0.20

→ 90% recall
→ 70% precision
→ 38% of all traffic flagged

That could be operationally terrible.

You need to connect:

Threshold → Recall → Precision → Alert volume

⸻

4. Business cost / expected loss

This is the biggest missing piece.

Instead of asking only:

“Where is F1 highest?”

Ask:

“What threshold minimizes expected business loss?”

For example:

Cost of missed ATO
+
Cost of false positive
+
Cost of manual investigation
+
Cost of customer friction

Then select the threshold that produces the best business outcome.

This follows the important distinction between prediction and decision-making: the best model metric isn’t automatically the best business operating point.

⸻

5. Calibration should be done on independent data

If you decide to calibrate the probabilities, don’t calibrate using the same data used to train the model. Independent/calibration data or appropriate cross-validation is important to avoid biased probability estimates.

